.\"                                      Hey, EMACS: -*- nroff -*-
.TH ADAM-MOSHCP 1 "May 2026"
.SH NAME
adam-moshcp \- transfer files over an active Mosh session
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B adam-moshcp
.B send
[\fIoptions\fP]
\fISOURCE\fP...
.br
.B adam-moshcp
.B receive
[\fIoptions\fP]
[\fIDEST\fP|\fIDESTDIR\fP]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBadam-moshcp\fP transfers files over an already-running
.BR adam-mosh (1)
session. It uses a local Unix-domain control socket exposed by
\fBadam-mosh-client\fP or \fBadam-mosh-server\fP, then sends bulk datagrams over
the existing encrypted and authenticated Mosh UDP connection.

\fBadam-moshcp\fP is intended for hostile or expensive networks where a
small, paced, forward-error-corrected transfer is preferable to opening
a second transport. The default rate is conservative so terminal
traffic remains usable while a transfer is running.

Run \fBadam-moshcp receive\fP on one side of the Mosh session and
\fBadam-moshcp send\fP on the other. The receiver writes to a temporary file
and renames it into place after the file has been decoded. Multiple
files and recursive directories are sent as one adam-moshcp archive payload.

.SH EXAMPLES

.nf
remote$ adam-moshcp receive .
local$  adam-moshcp send --rate=2k --redundancy=20% ./archive.tar
.fi

.nf
remote$ adam-moshcp receive /tmp/output.bin
local$  adam-moshcp send --fec=raptorq --rate=1500 ./output.bin
.fi

.nf
remote$ adam-moshcp receive --multi incoming
local$  adam-moshcp send -r -p -z --zstd-level=22 --rate=1500 photos notes.txt
.fi

.SH COMMANDS

.TP
.B send
Read one or more \fISOURCE\fP paths, split the payload into FEC
blocks, and send the manifest, source symbols, and repair symbols.
For backward compatibility, a two-argument send where the second path
does not exist is treated as a single source with a destination
basename. New scripts should use
.B \-\-dest\-name
for that form.

.TP
.B receive
Wait for a manifest, collect symbols, request extra repair symbols
when decoding fails, and write the completed file. If \fIDEST\fP names
an existing directory or ends in a slash, the sender's basename is
created inside that directory. Otherwise \fIDEST\fP is used as the
output path.

.SH OPTIONS

.TP
.B \-\-socket=\fIPATH\fP
Connect to a specific adam-moshcp control socket. By default,
\fBadam-moshcp\fP uses
.B ADAM_MOSHCP_SOCK
if it is set, otherwise the latest adam-moshcp control socket in the
user's runtime directory.

.TP
.B \-\-fec=reed-solomon|raptorq
Select the FEC codec. The default is \fBreed-solomon\fP.
\fBraptorq\fP requires a build configured with libRaptorQ support.

.TP
.B \-\-redundancy=\fIPERCENT\fP
Send this percentage of repair symbols in the initial pass. The
default is 20%. The receiver can still request extra repair symbols
after the initial pass.

.TP
.B \-\-rate=\fIBYTES\fP
Limit bulk send rate. A value of 0 disables the sender-side pacer. The
default is 2048 bytes per second. The suffixes \fBK\fP, \fBM\fP, and
\fBG\fP multiply by powers of 1024.

.TP
.B \-\-block-size=\fIBYTES\fP
Set the FEC block size. The default is 32768 bytes.

.TP
.B \-\-symbol-size=\fIBYTES\fP
Set the FEC symbol size and approximate payload size for symbol
datagrams. The default is 512 bytes.

.TP
.B \-\-transfer\-id=\fIHEX\fP
Use or accept only a specific transfer id. This is useful when several
adam-moshcp transfers share the same Mosh session and a receiver should be
pinned to one sender.

.TP
.B \-\-multi
Keep
.B adam-moshcp receive
running after one transfer completes and track multiple simultaneous
transfer ids. Without this option, the receiver exits after the first
completed transfer.

.TP
.B \-r, \-\-recursive
Allow directory sources. Directories are walked recursively and sent as
a adam-moshcp archive payload.

.TP
.B \-p, \-\-preserve
Preserve file modes and modification times when receiving. Without
this option, files are created using normal local permissions and
current timestamps.

.TP
.B \-\-dest\-name=\fINAME\fP
Set the destination basename for a single source. For a directory
source, this renames the top-level archived directory.

.TP
.B \-z, \-\-zstd
Compress the payload with zstd before FEC encoding. This requires a
build configured with libzstd support.

.TP
.B \-\-zstd\-level=\fIN\fP
Set the zstd compression level and enable zstd compression. Levels 0
through 22 are accepted; the default is 3. High levels can be useful on
very slow uplinks, but they are CPU and memory intensive.

.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Suppress periodic progress output. Errors are still reported.

.SH DISCOVERY
\fBadam-mosh-client\fP and \fBadam-mosh-server\fP expose a local control socket
and set
.B ADAM_MOSHCP_SOCK
for processes they start when possible. A fallback symlink named
\fBadam-moshcp.latest\fP is also created in the user's runtime directory.

This discovery model is intentionally simple. It can point at the
wrong Mosh connection if a shell is preserved by a multiplexer such as
\fBtmux\fP after one Mosh client disconnects and another connects. This
is similar to stale SSH agent forwarding state; use
\fB\-\-socket\fP when the automatic choice is ambiguous.

.SH NETWORK BEHAVIOR
The manifest describes the file name, size, mode, mtime, FEC codec,
block size, symbol size, compression settings, archive mode, and
transfer id. Symbol datagrams carry one FEC symbol. ACK datagrams
report decoded block ranges, request additional repair symbols, and
carry lightweight counters for received symbols, duplicate symbols,
and requested repair symbols. Finish datagrams report final status.

Bulk datagrams are separate from terminal state datagrams but use the
same Mosh encryption and authentication. Mosh sends bulk output only
when no reliable terminal or stream-forwarding data is queued, and
schedules it conservatively so large transfers do not intentionally
fill slow queues. If a bulk datagram is larger than the current Mosh
path-MTU payload budget, Mosh rejects it instead of handing it to UDP
fragmentation; reduce
.B \-\-symbol\-size
if this happens.

